# Git commits — explicit request only

Never create a git commit unless the user explicitly asks using clear commit language, such as:

- "commit"
- "create a commit"
- "make a commit"
- "commit these changes"

Do **not** treat any of the following as a commit request:

- "clean up"
- "wrap up"
- "ship it"
- "looks good"
- "we're done"
- "finalize"
- "push" (push ≠ commit; only push when explicitly asked)

If commit intent is unclear, ask first and do **not** commit.

When an explicit commit is requested, follow the repo's normal git safety protocol (no force push, no amend unless allowed, no secrets, etc.).
